  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nA weak frontal boundary will continue to move through into the \nmorning hours from northwest to southeast. This will bring \nadditional light rainfall to areas mainly along and west of the \nmountains. HRRR shows some spots may see rain rates near one third \nof an inch per hour as the boundary moves through. NBM paints a \ngeneral 25-40% chance of seeing over 0.25\" in total from this when \nall is said and done by mid morning. Hi-res guidance shows some \nshowers may stick around the San Diego metro and mountain areas into \nSaturday afternoon. Otherwise, most areas west of the mountains will \nstart to clear from north to south with the best chance of clouds \nlingering in western San Diego county into the early evening. A lull \nin the action is expected into Sunday as a weak ridge moves over the \narea. This will bring sunnier and drier conditions with highs a bit \nwarmer with temperatures near to around 5 degrees below seasonal \naverages.\n\nAnother low pressure system will swoop by southwestern British \nColumbia on Sunday. Models indicate a trough from this system \ncutting off from the mean flow and pushing southward over our region \non Monday and Tuesday. There still remains some slight differences \nin timing but chances for precipitation will increase at first near \nthe coast Monday morning with better chances into the valleys and \nmountains by Monday evening through sometime on Tuesday. As \nmentioned in yesterday's discussion, model guidance continues to \nshow a drying trend with this system indicating lighter rain and \nhigh mountain snow totals. Some resort towns may be able to see \nlight snow on Tuesday morning as the system passes through, but this \nstill remains preliminary.\n\nA drier, albeit short, period of weather will occur later on Tuesday \ninto the first half of Wednesday. Yet another low pressure system \nwill move across the State later in the week. GFS/ECMWF solutions \nare beginning to show a better start time with precipitation with \nthis system, beginning sometime later Wednesday evening or Thursday \nmorning. How far south the system goes is still in question but \nsolutions are beginning to show signs of it moving closer to our \nregion, making it more favorable for more spells of wet weather. \nConfidence is growing that this will bring more colder air and \ngreater amounts of precipitation than the system on Monday/Tuesday. \nThough amounts are still uncertain, this system has an increased \nchance to bring snowfall to some of our higher mountain communities. \nModel guidance begins to diverge on the ending time of the \nrain/mountain snow, some models keeping the low pressure system \naround until later on Thursday or even Friday of next week.\n\nTemperatures will be fairly similar this week with near average \nreadings at the coast and cooler than normal for inland areas, \nespecially as we progress deeper into next week. As these weather \nsystems each move near the area, expect breezy onshore winds at the \nimmediate coast, mountains, and deserts to accompany them.",
